5. Vibe replies

Replies enable conversations around individual Vibes.

5.1 Reply capabilities

Users should be able to:

- Send a text reply.
- View permitted replies.
- Edit their own reply where supported by Arvdoul's messaging conventions.
- Delete their own reply.
- Report inappropriate replies.
- See reply timestamps.
- See appropriate sender identity.

Reply interactions must be available only when the creator's audience and interaction permissions allow them.

5.2 Reply permissions

Support configurable reply policies:

- Everyone eligible to view.
- Followers.
- Connections.
- Close Circle.
- Nobody.

The policy must be evaluated by the backend.

Do not assume viewing permission automatically grants reply permission.

5.3 Reply data

Logical model:

interface VibeReply {
  id: string;
  vibeId: string;

  senderId: string;
  recipientId: string;

  text: string;

  createdAt: Timestamp;
  updatedAt?: Timestamp;

  status: "active" | "deleted" | "hidden";
}

Adapt this to the existing message architecture if Vibe replies are implemented as message objects.

5.4 Reply experience

The Viewer should provide a compact reply entry point.

Design requirements:

- Contextual reply field.
- Appropriate keyboard behavior.
- Clear send action.
- Loading feedback.
- Sending state.
- Error recovery.
- Character limit.
- Accessible controls.
- Safe-area support.
- Smooth keyboard transitions.

Do not allow the reply interface to obscure the Vibe unnecessarily.

5.5 Messaging integration

Vibe replies should connect to the creator's direct conversation with the viewer using the existing messaging architecture.

The system should preserve:

- Sender identity.
- Recipient identity.
- Vibe reference.
- Reply content.
- Timestamp.
- Conversation context.

If the creator cannot receive a message because of privacy or blocking restrictions, handle the reply action accordingly.

Do not create a separate conversation system solely for Vibes.


